  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">FTF Bangladesh Sustainable Aquaculture Activity</narrative>
  </title>
  <description>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">(Bangladesh):  The FTF Bangladesh Sustainable Aquaculture Activity coordinates linkages among value chain actors across private, public, and civil society spheres. With FY 2024 funds, the activity will develop the capacity of hatchery owners, nursery owners, fish fingerling sellers, fish feed manufacturers, feed dealers, and a broad range of other industry support services. Supported firms will continue to grow and contribute to building the capacity of all aquaculture value chain actors, including women, within the southern regions of Bangladesh. The activity&apos;s technical assistance will improve productivity, production, and quality of aquaculture systems and strengthen market systems with expanding opportunities for women and youth. Through targeted messaging and technical service on diversification of household production, smallholder aquaculture households will gain greater awareness to adopt nutrition-enhancing behaviors. The activity will promote awareness and adoption of improved nutrition behaviors and practices in rural communities through increased fish consumption. Value chain actors will also receive training on improved production practices and technologies and sub-grants to pilot and scale-up successful innovations. In addition, the activity will test and promote climate-smart technologies to ensure efficient aquaculture practices. The activity aligns with the Global Food Security Strategy and significantly contributes to achieving the State-USAID Joint Strategic Plan goal two. Funding for the activity contributes to Adaptation-Indirect ($500k), Global Food Security (1.17m), Gender Equality/Women Empowerment-Secondary ($500k), Microenterprises and Livelihoods ($100,000), Multi-Sectoral Nutrition ($1.05m), Innovation ($100k), Private Sector Engagement ($300k), Small and Medium Sized Enterprises ($200k), and Women Owned Small and Medium Sized Enterprises ($100k) earmarks and key issues.</narrative>
  </description>
